WEBVTT

00:01.500 --> 00:04.743
♪ It seems today
that all you see ♪

00:04.876 --> 00:08.246
♪ Is violence in movies
and sex on TV ♪

00:08.280 --> 00:11.599
♪ But where are those good
old-fashioned values ♪

00:11.634 --> 00:14.453
♪ On which we used to rely?

00:14.487 --> 00:17.741
♪ Lucky there's a family guy

00:17.775 --> 00:21.495
♪ Lucky there's a man who
positively can do ♪

00:21.546 --> 00:23.297
♪ All the things that make us

00:23.332 --> 00:24.782
♪ Laugh and cry

00:24.816 --> 00:26.317
♪ He's... a...
fam... ily... guy! ♪

00:35.814 --> 00:37.882
Wow, I'm so excited
for Brian.

00:37.917 --> 00:39.551
Remember, kids--
if it's terrible,

00:39.585 --> 00:42.171
at the end, we all say,
"You did it!"

00:42.205 --> 00:43.673
I can't believe we're
going to the theater

00:43.707 --> 00:46.076
the same day Chris
drowned a mouse in a puddle.

00:46.110 --> 00:48.645
I mean, don't we need a day
to clear our heads?

00:48.663 --> 00:49.830
Boy, this takes me back.

00:49.864 --> 00:51.032
You know, I was, uh...

00:51.083 --> 00:52.900
kind of one of those theater
kids in high school.

00:52.935 --> 00:55.621
♪ Suddenly Seymour...

00:55.655 --> 00:57.639
♪ Suddenly Seymour...

00:57.674 --> 00:59.609
♪ He purified me

00:59.643 --> 01:01.961
♪ He purified you

01:01.996 --> 01:03.931
♪ Suddenly Seymour...

01:03.965 --> 01:06.867
♪ Suddenly Seymour...

01:06.902 --> 01:09.738
Mrs. Andrews, I pooped
in the pot again.

01:09.772 --> 01:12.041
I'm gonna need somebody
to clean me up.

01:12.076 --> 01:13.242
(crying)

01:13.277 --> 01:15.578
It's really bad this time.

01:15.612 --> 01:17.047
Wow, what a turnout.

01:17.082 --> 01:18.332
Oh, hey, there's Glenn.

01:18.366 --> 01:19.349
Hey, guys.

01:19.367 --> 01:20.719
Hey, this is my date,
Consuela.

01:20.753 --> 01:22.737
No, no, no.

01:22.772 --> 01:24.323
Okay, we're here
as friends,

01:24.357 --> 01:26.525
but I'm gonna change
your mind one day.

01:26.560 --> 01:29.730
(indistinct conversations)

01:29.764 --> 01:31.364
Oh, I'm so proud of Brian.

01:31.399 --> 01:33.084
It's hard to believe
he's the same dog

01:33.118 --> 01:35.470
who barked at a pineapple
for four hours.

01:35.504 --> 01:37.706
Close your purse.
I can see your tampons.

01:37.741 --> 01:39.208
And why do you need six?

01:39.242 --> 01:41.211
What happens to you?

01:41.245 --> 01:42.562
There's the man
of the hour.

01:42.596 --> 01:43.713
Hey, Brian, can you believe

01:43.748 --> 01:45.783
I found this blazer
on the side of the highway?

01:45.817 --> 01:47.284
Hey, guys.
Thanks for coming.

01:47.319 --> 01:49.553
Big night, Brian.
How you feeling?

01:49.588 --> 01:50.555
Well, a little nervous.

01:50.590 --> 01:51.740
I just want it to go well.

01:51.774 --> 01:52.791
Fingers crossed.

01:52.825 --> 01:54.093
Will there be
an intermission?

01:54.128 --> 01:55.728
Because that will determine
whether or not

01:55.763 --> 01:58.164
I bring this empty Gatorade
bottle into the theater.

01:58.199 --> 01:59.566
TOM: This is
unacceptable!

01:59.601 --> 02:01.118
Sorry, guys,
I got to go.

02:01.169 --> 02:03.388
They didn't put masking tape
over a seat for Tom Tucker

02:03.422 --> 02:05.073
and he's in there
pitching a fit.

02:05.107 --> 02:06.147
TOM: Do you
know who I am?!

02:06.158 --> 02:07.476
I'm Tom Tucker,
damn it!

02:07.511 --> 02:10.713
I make more in an hour
than you make in two hours!

02:10.747 --> 02:13.066
Oh, the play's about to start.

02:14.051 --> 02:15.753
Mom, am I going to fit
in the seat

02:15.771 --> 02:17.939
or is this going to be
like last time?

02:18.991 --> 02:20.909
This should tide you
over for a while.

02:20.944 --> 02:23.412
I'll come back
as soon as I can.

02:25.299 --> 02:27.183
She's not coming
back, you know.

02:27.218 --> 02:28.419
How do you know that?

02:28.453 --> 02:30.488
'Cause she's
my mom, too.

02:32.875 --> 02:35.911
Excuse me, that's my seat.

02:37.130 --> 02:39.365
We'll talk
about this at home.

02:40.400 --> 02:41.435
(sighs):
Let's see.

02:41.469 --> 02:42.436
Who's in this thing?

02:42.470 --> 02:43.470
Sad. Nobody.

02:43.505 --> 02:45.273
Sad. Sad. Nobody.

02:45.307 --> 02:46.374
Victor Garber?

02:46.408 --> 02:47.475
That's pretty good.

02:47.510 --> 02:48.810
How did he get Victor...?

02:48.844 --> 02:49.979
Oh, of course.

02:50.013 --> 02:51.931
Tonight it's Randall Evan
Battincourt.

02:51.965 --> 02:53.400
God, I hate understudies.

02:53.434 --> 02:54.634
King Louis the Sixteenth

02:54.669 --> 02:57.370
has been sentenced to death
by guillotine.

02:57.405 --> 02:58.856
(cheering)

02:58.891 --> 03:00.558
The part of King Louis
the Sixteenth

03:00.592 --> 03:03.195
will be played
by Jean de Menard.

03:03.229 --> 03:04.663
(crowd groans)

03:04.697 --> 03:06.332
Relax, you're
going to do great.

03:06.367 --> 03:07.500
(sighs)

03:09.703 --> 03:10.855
Nailed it.

03:14.927 --> 03:16.094
Well, as usual,

03:16.128 --> 03:18.263
this is gonna suck
for one of us.

03:19.950 --> 03:22.285
Donna? It's Grant,
your new husband.

03:22.319 --> 03:23.420
Hi, honey.

03:23.454 --> 03:24.855
I've got great news.

03:24.889 --> 03:26.006
What is it?

03:26.040 --> 03:27.909
First, where do we keep
the good scotch?

03:27.943 --> 03:29.577
At your brother's house.

03:29.612 --> 03:31.814
(audience laughing,
Peter laughing hard)

03:31.848 --> 03:34.683
I got an audition for the
hottest new play in town.

03:34.718 --> 03:36.553
This could really
make my career

03:36.587 --> 03:38.521
and we can finally
start our family.

03:38.556 --> 03:39.656
What's it called?

03:39.690 --> 03:40.992
<i>A Passing Fancy.</i>

03:41.026 --> 03:42.126
(audience gasps softly)

03:42.160 --> 03:44.095
(gasps)
That's the name of this play.

03:44.129 --> 03:46.565
How did he do that?
How did he do that?!

03:48.101 --> 03:49.235
Here, Donna.

03:49.270 --> 03:51.237
I got you your ten-year
anniversary present.

03:51.272 --> 03:52.605
Suntan lotion?

03:52.640 --> 03:54.074
But we live
in the Northeast.

03:54.109 --> 03:55.776
That's right, we do.

03:55.810 --> 03:58.847
Unless we were moving
to California.

03:58.881 --> 04:00.749
(gasps)
He must've got the part.

04:00.783 --> 04:02.618
When he going
to meet Evita?

04:02.653 --> 04:04.654
<i>Not every play is Evita.</i>

04:04.688 --> 04:06.723
Oh, no.
I feel terrible.

04:06.758 --> 04:08.425
Why?
Don't you want to go?

04:08.459 --> 04:11.479
No-- I got you
a parka.

04:11.513 --> 04:12.830
(laughing)

04:12.865 --> 04:16.101
Those two are never
on the same page.

04:17.137 --> 04:19.172
Wait for the look...

04:19.206 --> 04:21.474
(audience laughing)

04:21.509 --> 04:23.778
And that's why it works.

04:25.314 --> 04:26.882
(crying)

04:26.916 --> 04:28.517
Donna, don't go!

04:28.551 --> 04:31.354
It's not too late.
We can still start our family.

04:31.388 --> 04:32.388
Don't you see?

04:32.423 --> 04:34.123
You already have
a family.

04:34.158 --> 04:35.659
It's right there.

04:35.694 --> 04:37.795
(audience "oohs")

04:37.829 --> 04:39.965
Those won't keep you
warm at night.

04:39.999 --> 04:41.599
Good-bye, Grant.

04:41.634 --> 04:42.968
It's funny, isn't it?

04:43.002 --> 04:46.872
All these years, I was
the passing fancy.

04:49.410 --> 04:52.045
(cheering)

04:56.886 --> 04:59.805
Get up, you jerk.

04:59.839 --> 05:01.841
(cheering)

05:11.938 --> 05:13.823
(cheering continues)

05:16.110 --> 05:17.527
Wow, that was amazing!

05:17.578 --> 05:19.646
I mean, usually,
the shows suck in this town.

05:19.664 --> 05:22.333
Like Moishe, the low-energy
Bar Mitzvah clown.

05:22.368 --> 05:24.519
Ooh, what's this?

05:24.553 --> 05:25.587
A quarter.

05:25.621 --> 05:27.723
I found it behind
your ear earlier.

05:27.758 --> 05:29.725
All right, that's enough
tricks for you.

05:29.760 --> 05:32.262
I'm going to go lie
down on the coats.

05:37.569 --> 05:39.254
Hey, has anybody seen
the review yet?

05:39.289 --> 05:40.406
No, we've been waiting for you.

05:40.440 --> 05:41.390
Thanks.

05:41.424 --> 05:43.126
Oh, here it is.

05:43.160 --> 05:46.996
<i>"Here's hoping A Passing Fancy
is actually a staying fancy.</i>

05:47.031 --> 05:49.967
"The highest praise goes
to playwright Brian Griffin

05:50.001 --> 05:51.903
"for his hilarious
and insightful look

05:51.938 --> 05:53.338
"into modern relationships.

05:53.372 --> 05:55.741
"If you see only one play
as an adult,

05:55.775 --> 05:57.443
I urge you to see this one."

05:57.478 --> 05:58.745
Hey, can you read
that page

05:58.779 --> 06:00.664
with all the little paragraphs
about dead people?

06:00.698 --> 06:02.032
Those are hilarious.

06:02.067 --> 06:03.984
This is amazing, Brian.
How do you feel?

06:04.018 --> 06:05.553
You're a hit!

06:05.588 --> 06:06.855
It's... overwhelming.

06:06.889 --> 06:08.991
This is all I've ever wanted,
you know?

06:09.025 --> 06:11.811
For people to appreciate
and respect my writing.

06:11.845 --> 06:13.413
I want to write
a play!

06:13.448 --> 06:14.681
(chuckles)
Aw, that's cute, Stewie.

06:14.715 --> 06:15.882
Maybe someday.

06:15.917 --> 06:16.983
No, I'm going
to write a play.

06:17.019 --> 06:18.352
I can do lots
of things.

06:18.387 --> 06:20.755
I was the human resources
director for the Muppets.

06:20.789 --> 06:22.858
Mr. Beaker, it is my
unfortunate task

06:22.892 --> 06:24.393
to inform you
that your services

06:24.427 --> 06:26.529
will no longer be required
by this company.

06:26.564 --> 06:28.298
(unintelligible squeak)

06:28.332 --> 06:29.666
Well, if you must know,

06:29.700 --> 06:31.335
it's because we've
noticed large quantities

06:31.369 --> 06:32.903
of benzene, ephedrine

06:32.938 --> 06:34.973
and everyday Epsom salt
have gone missing.

06:35.007 --> 06:36.274
(unintelligible squeak)

06:36.309 --> 06:37.309
Oh, please.

06:37.343 --> 06:38.511
We both know those
are the ingredients

06:38.545 --> 06:40.012
used to make
crystal meth.

06:40.047 --> 06:41.414
And I've got
a pretty good idea

06:41.448 --> 06:43.083
who you've been
selling it to as well.

06:43.118 --> 06:44.351
Oh, hey, uh, Beaker,

06:44.385 --> 06:45.752
when you're done
talking to your friend,

06:45.770 --> 06:46.921
I want to, uh, eh...

06:46.956 --> 06:48.656
I want to talk to you
about that thing.

06:51.661 --> 06:52.912
Hey, what you doing?

06:52.946 --> 06:54.713
I was going to start
rehearsing my new play.

06:54.748 --> 06:55.916
Want to hear it?

06:55.950 --> 06:57.834
Oh, you actually wrote
a little play.

06:57.869 --> 06:59.837
Aw, boy, I wish
I could hang out,

06:59.872 --> 07:02.023
but it has just
gotten so crazy.

07:02.057 --> 07:03.675
Really?
I know, right?

07:03.710 --> 07:05.410
I mean, I'm not
complaining, you know?

07:05.445 --> 07:07.479
But I just... I want
my old life back.

07:07.514 --> 07:08.731
(jokingly growls)

07:08.766 --> 07:09.799
I'm just kidding,
of course.

07:09.834 --> 07:11.534
I'm so grateful,
you know?

07:11.569 --> 07:13.420
I mean, every
performance is sold out

07:13.455 --> 07:14.889
and they just added
a Wednesday matinee.

07:14.923 --> 07:16.307
It's great.

07:16.341 --> 07:17.676
I mean, there's only
one thing I'm not getting

07:17.710 --> 07:18.677
out of this experience.

07:18.711 --> 07:19.878
What's that?
Sleep.

07:19.912 --> 07:20.947
(jokingly growls)

07:20.981 --> 07:22.398
No, no, I'm so
grateful, really.

07:22.432 --> 07:24.300
It's just that I'm-I'm
so busy all the time.

07:24.334 --> 07:25.569
But it's great.
It's all great.

07:25.603 --> 07:27.404
Well, do you think you might
have time to read it?

07:27.438 --> 07:28.605
(sighs)
All right, all right.

07:28.640 --> 07:29.891
But be patient,
all right?

07:29.925 --> 07:31.526
I've got interviews and
appearances all week.

07:31.560 --> 07:32.760
I mean, they're squeezing

07:32.795 --> 07:34.157
every last drop they
can out of me,

07:34.164 --> 07:35.698
like a bottle of ketchup.

07:37.250 --> 07:39.119
Listen, we've been at this
for 15 minutes.

07:39.153 --> 07:40.737
I don't think anything's
coming out.

07:40.771 --> 07:42.373
I'm fine.
I can keep going.

07:42.407 --> 07:43.824
I'm just really tired.

07:43.859 --> 07:45.659
What if I stick my finger
in your hole?

07:45.677 --> 07:47.996
Eh, that only works
when I'm already close.

07:51.619 --> 07:53.686
The American play was dying.

07:53.721 --> 07:56.156
Have-have we brought it
back to life here?

07:56.191 --> 07:58.726
I can't say that,
but it has a pulse.

07:58.760 --> 08:00.829
You're such an amazing
writer, Brian.

08:00.863 --> 08:02.964
How do you do it?
What's your process?

08:02.999 --> 08:05.818
Oh, God, Allison,
how do I even answer that?

08:05.852 --> 08:08.622
Uh, I mean, what's
a rainbow's process?

08:08.656 --> 08:12.660
Two parts rain, one part sun,
one part childlike wonder.

08:12.695 --> 08:14.128
What we do know, though,

08:14.163 --> 08:17.466
is that the end result
in both cases is joy.

08:19.169 --> 08:20.487
Wow.

08:20.521 --> 08:22.455
Wow, are you glad you left
the lighthouse now, Seamus?

08:22.490 --> 08:23.957
Oh, I think so, Seamus!

08:23.991 --> 08:26.010
Excuse me, I noticed
you were talking loud,

08:26.044 --> 08:27.612
so that means you're smart.

08:27.646 --> 08:29.548
But would you mind--
and I hate asking--

08:29.582 --> 08:31.183
but would you mind
signing this?

08:31.217 --> 08:32.952
Is this guy giving you
a hard time, Brian?

08:32.987 --> 08:33.987
Nah, he's all right.

08:34.021 --> 08:35.088
All right. Cool, cool.

08:35.122 --> 08:36.389
How you doing, buddy?

08:51.476 --> 08:54.462
(Mozart's 29th Symphony
in A Major plays)

09:13.069 --> 09:15.305
(music builds)

09:28.021 --> 09:31.524
(music ends)

09:31.558 --> 09:33.093
My God.

09:33.127 --> 09:35.896
It's miraculous.

09:35.930 --> 09:38.399
(bleep)

09:38.434 --> 09:40.118
Hey, Brian, can you
read my play?

09:40.152 --> 09:41.320
(fart noise)

09:41.354 --> 09:43.472
Sorry, there was a fart
trapped in the play.

09:49.906 --> 09:51.340
Hey, Jasper, how are you?

09:51.374 --> 09:52.341
Hey, Brian!

09:52.375 --> 09:54.210
Congratulations
on your new play.

09:54.245 --> 09:55.445
Oh, thanks.

09:55.479 --> 09:56.980
What's wrong?
You look awful.

09:57.014 --> 09:58.149
What happened?

09:58.183 --> 09:59.617
I read the play Stewie wrote.

09:59.651 --> 10:01.185
She writes plays now?

10:01.219 --> 10:02.921
Yes, and it was brilliant.

10:02.955 --> 10:05.524
Jasper, I've never read anything
like it in my life.

10:05.558 --> 10:08.077
It was insightful and fresh
and intelligent.

10:08.111 --> 10:10.012
It's like his play
is mocking me.

10:10.047 --> 10:11.582
He wrote it in a night.

10:11.616 --> 10:14.736
Ugh, that's how I feel whenever
I see Brad Goreski on Bravo.

10:14.770 --> 10:16.704
God, everyone's on Bravo but me.

10:16.739 --> 10:19.275
Anyhoo, have you told him
how good it is?

10:19.309 --> 10:20.509
No. I just read it.

10:20.527 --> 10:22.194
He gave it to me
'cause he wanted my feedback.

10:22.229 --> 10:23.363
Okay, listen to me.

10:23.397 --> 10:25.498
Tell Stewie it's awful,
it's garbage.

10:25.533 --> 10:27.000
Do whatever
you have to do, Brian,

10:27.034 --> 10:28.769
but never let him know
he's got talent.

10:28.804 --> 10:29.871
Yeah, you're right.

10:29.905 --> 10:31.405
It won't be so hard.

10:31.440 --> 10:33.342
After all, I've
certainly done worse.

10:33.376 --> 10:35.477
I replaced Peter's
I Can't Believe It's Not Butter

10:35.511 --> 10:36.946
with real butter.

10:39.617 --> 10:42.519
Buh... wuh... suh... muh-wuh...

10:42.554 --> 10:45.290
I... I can't...
I can't believe...

10:45.324 --> 10:47.125
Muh... wuh...

10:47.159 --> 10:48.426
(screaming)

10:48.477 --> 10:49.896
I don't know, Doctor.

10:49.930 --> 10:52.231
Looking back, I think it
may have been real butter.

10:52.266 --> 10:54.651
Your husband murdered
three children.

10:57.973 --> 11:00.008
Oh, my God.
Look at this.

11:00.042 --> 11:01.577
Brian, get in here.

11:01.611 --> 11:02.611
What?

11:02.662 --> 11:04.296
Your play has
been nominated

11:04.331 --> 11:08.001
for a local Hoggy Award
for Best Creative Anything.

11:08.035 --> 11:09.419
Oh, that's great.

11:09.453 --> 11:11.856
Um, I'm gonna...

11:11.890 --> 11:14.176
I'm going to go get some air.

11:15.795 --> 11:16.828
Look at that.

11:16.863 --> 11:18.230
Nominated
for an award.

11:18.265 --> 11:19.816
What have you
two done today?

11:19.850 --> 11:22.151
I found a lump
under one of my balls.

11:22.186 --> 11:23.687
Okay, that's
something.

11:23.722 --> 11:25.789
I ate three quarters
of an Entenmann's strudel.

11:25.824 --> 11:28.326
Youse are good kids.

11:31.498 --> 11:34.917
Hey, so did you get
a chance to read my play?

11:34.952 --> 11:36.453
Uh... I did.

11:36.487 --> 11:37.587
I did.

11:37.622 --> 11:38.689
Ooh, I'm so nervous.

11:38.723 --> 11:39.991
What'd you think?

11:40.025 --> 11:41.509
Stewie, you and I
are friends, right?

11:41.543 --> 11:42.493
Yeah, I mean,
I know you

11:42.528 --> 11:43.661
through our mutual
friend Greg,

11:43.696 --> 11:45.431
but sure, I'd say
we're friends now.

11:45.465 --> 11:46.715
Exactly. And-and
as your friend,

11:46.750 --> 11:48.585
you'd want me to be
honest with you, right?

11:48.619 --> 11:49.619
Totally.

11:49.654 --> 11:50.820
It wasn't good.

11:50.855 --> 11:52.155
Oh, no, really?

11:52.190 --> 11:54.458
Oh, I worked
so hard on it.

11:54.493 --> 11:57.529
Oh, I thought it
was good. Aw.

11:57.563 --> 12:00.683
Oh, I know, I wanted it
to be good so much.

12:00.734 --> 12:02.518
Oh, I am so bummed out
right now.

12:02.553 --> 12:04.721
I-I... Now I don't even know
if I should submit it.

12:04.755 --> 12:06.390
Wha... Submit it?

12:06.424 --> 12:07.708
Yeah, I was planning
to submit it

12:07.742 --> 12:09.210
to the Roundabout Theater
Company in New York.

12:09.245 --> 12:10.445
No! I mean...

12:10.479 --> 12:12.119
Oh, I mean, that-that
would be a bad idea.

12:12.148 --> 12:13.516
Definitely not.

12:13.550 --> 12:15.818
No, no, they won't let
you down easy like I did.

12:15.852 --> 12:18.522
New York can be pretty harsh,
especially to outsiders.

12:18.556 --> 12:20.490
Hello, New York!

12:20.525 --> 12:22.410
(dragon screeching)

12:22.444 --> 12:24.579
Ugh, maybe you're right.
I'll think about it.

12:24.613 --> 12:27.199
Anyway, can I have that back?
It's my only copy.

12:27.233 --> 12:28.434
Your only copy?

12:28.468 --> 12:29.518
Yes.

12:29.552 --> 12:30.837
And thank you
for being a good friend

12:30.871 --> 12:32.138
and telling me
the truth, Brian.

12:32.173 --> 12:33.573
I know that was
hard for you.

12:33.607 --> 12:35.876
And to show my appreciation
for reading my play,

12:35.911 --> 12:37.712
I made you this.

12:39.082 --> 12:40.282
Wow, "good dog."

12:40.316 --> 12:41.783
And it's official?

12:41.818 --> 12:43.202
That's what it says,
doesn't it?

12:43.237 --> 12:45.555
I wish my dad was
alive to see this.

13:01.342 --> 13:02.476
Hey.

13:02.510 --> 13:03.660
Who the hell
are you?

13:03.694 --> 13:05.630
I'm the guy Stewie pays
to watch him sleep.

13:05.664 --> 13:06.697
Who are you?

13:06.732 --> 13:08.299
I'm Brian.
I live here.

13:08.333 --> 13:09.601
Oh.

13:09.652 --> 13:11.270
Do you mind if
I take this script?

13:11.304 --> 13:12.538
Yeah, sure, whatever.

13:12.572 --> 13:14.057
He just pays me
to watch him sleep

13:14.091 --> 13:16.493
and rub this knife
across my face while I do it.

13:16.527 --> 13:18.195
Oh.

13:18.230 --> 13:19.947
Okay.

13:21.667 --> 13:23.452
Hey, how much
does Stewie pay you?

13:23.503 --> 13:25.120
$4,800 a week.

13:25.171 --> 13:26.255
(bleep)

13:26.290 --> 13:27.473
Yeah.

13:31.112 --> 13:32.362
Hey, Brian.

13:32.397 --> 13:33.430
Oh, hey, Stewie.

13:33.465 --> 13:34.799
Listen, have you
seen my play?

13:34.834 --> 13:36.634
You know, Stewie,
I have seen your play,

13:36.669 --> 13:39.105
and it is
exhilarating to me.

13:39.139 --> 13:41.073
A child's play is one of

13:41.108 --> 13:42.793
the most beautiful
things in the world.

13:42.827 --> 13:44.761
Continue to play,
little one.

13:44.796 --> 13:46.363
You're our future.

13:46.397 --> 13:48.166
What the (bleep) are
you talking about?

13:48.200 --> 13:49.801
The play I wrote.
Have you seen it?

13:49.835 --> 13:51.687
Uh, I don't know.
Let me call my lawyer.

13:51.721 --> 13:53.238
(chuckles) Hello. I'm being
cross-examined.

13:53.273 --> 13:55.375
No, I haven't...
I haven't seen it.

13:55.409 --> 13:57.449
Really? 'Cause I had it
sitting right on my table...

13:57.478 --> 13:59.111
I said I haven't
seen it, all right!

13:59.130 --> 14:00.865
And what does it matter?
It was terrible anyway!

14:00.899 --> 14:01.966
We've had this conversation!

14:02.000 --> 14:03.501
Do yourself a favor
and move on!

14:03.535 --> 14:05.370
Well, that's interesting, Brian,
because I found this

14:05.404 --> 14:08.090
buried in the backyard
next to your soup bone.

14:08.125 --> 14:09.192
(gasps)

14:09.226 --> 14:10.259
Stewie, I...

14:10.294 --> 14:11.894
You tried to destroy it,
didn't you?

14:11.929 --> 14:13.330
I knew my play was good.

14:13.364 --> 14:15.632
Just like I knew your play
was a mediocre patchwork

14:15.667 --> 14:17.969
of hackneyed ideas
and tired clich├йs.

14:18.003 --> 14:20.104
You have no idea how hard it was

14:20.139 --> 14:22.575
to sit in that theater
with all those braying hyenas.

14:22.609 --> 14:24.577
Couldn't you tell
something was up

14:24.611 --> 14:26.847
when Chris and the fat man
could follow the plot?

14:26.881 --> 14:30.084
<i>I mean, it took Peter a year
to figure out Stuart Little.</i>

14:31.119 --> 14:32.453
I just figured it out.

14:32.488 --> 14:33.655
"Stuart" means "mouse."

14:33.690 --> 14:35.757
No, Peter.

14:35.792 --> 14:37.626
"Little" means "mouse"?

14:37.661 --> 14:38.828
No, Peter.

14:38.863 --> 14:42.166
I feel so old
and in the way.

14:42.200 --> 14:43.517
It's still a good play!

14:43.552 --> 14:45.119
It's filled with terrible
double entendres,

14:45.153 --> 14:46.788
puns and stolen bits.

14:46.822 --> 14:48.423
<i>There's a line in there
from Seinfeld!</i>

14:48.457 --> 14:50.058
I never saw that episode!

14:50.092 --> 14:51.160
I have a voice.

14:51.195 --> 14:52.228
Do you understand that?

14:52.262 --> 14:54.030
A writer needs a voice,
and I have one.

14:54.064 --> 14:55.465
You don't.

14:55.500 --> 14:57.985
Your play panders to the lowest
common denominator, Brian.

14:58.019 --> 15:00.021
And it doesn't even
do that well.

15:00.055 --> 15:00.755
Shut up!

15:00.789 --> 15:02.123
May every person

15:02.157 --> 15:03.993
that laughs at your
sophomoric effort

15:04.027 --> 15:06.161
be a reminder
of your eternal mediocrity

15:06.195 --> 15:09.282
and pierce your heart
like a knife.

15:15.324 --> 15:16.959
(squirrel chittering)

15:18.161 --> 15:19.528
Come here, you bastard!

15:19.562 --> 15:22.365
(barking)

15:22.399 --> 15:23.416
You stink!

15:23.450 --> 15:24.752
And your play stinks!

15:24.786 --> 15:26.754
This ain't about me, is it?

15:26.788 --> 15:30.125
(sobbing)

15:30.159 --> 15:32.360
No.

15:32.395 --> 15:34.597
And I'm sorry...

15:34.631 --> 15:37.534
but nobody can ever know that.

15:37.569 --> 15:39.486
(bone cracking)

15:42.825 --> 15:44.759
Donna? It's Grant,
your new husband!

15:44.793 --> 15:45.794
Hi, honey.

15:45.829 --> 15:47.179
I've got
great news.

15:47.213 --> 15:48.213
What is it?

15:48.248 --> 15:50.116
First, where do we keep
the good scotch?

15:50.151 --> 15:51.651
At your
brother's house.

15:51.686 --> 15:54.288
(audience laughing,
Peter laughing hard)

15:54.323 --> 15:56.357
PETER: Lois, can I have
my birthday here?

15:56.391 --> 15:58.142
Seems to be going well.

15:58.176 --> 15:59.328
What do you want?

15:59.362 --> 16:00.979
Wanted to share
a bit of good news.

16:01.014 --> 16:02.749
I just found out
that my play

16:02.783 --> 16:04.751
is going to be
produced on Broadway.

16:04.785 --> 16:06.152
What?

16:06.186 --> 16:09.123
And I'm taking Randall
Evan Battincourt with me.

16:09.157 --> 16:10.524
Hi, Stewie.
I'll be right there.

16:10.558 --> 16:12.327
I just got to
finish this crap.

16:18.620 --> 16:20.338
Hey, I need you
to take me to New York

16:20.372 --> 16:22.023
for the opening night
of my play.

16:22.057 --> 16:23.108
(scoffs)
No way.

16:23.142 --> 16:24.827
Besides, I checked,
and there's no play

16:24.861 --> 16:26.695
by Stewie Griffin
opening in New York.

16:26.730 --> 16:28.011
I submitted it
under a pseudonym.

16:28.032 --> 16:30.267
They think it was written
by Tony Dovolani.

16:30.301 --> 16:32.870
I thought your pseudonym
was Gorgeous Randy Flamethrower.

16:32.905 --> 16:34.906
That's my dodge ball
pseudonym.

16:34.940 --> 16:36.274
Look, if you won't take me,

16:36.308 --> 16:38.429
I'm sure the New York Theater
League would send a car.

16:38.445 --> 16:40.112
New York Theater League? Why?

16:40.146 --> 16:42.315
Because they're throwing
a welcome dinner in my honor.

16:42.349 --> 16:43.416
Really?

16:43.451 --> 16:45.386
Well, I guess I could
clear my schedule.

16:45.420 --> 16:46.954
Oh, you won't
regret it, Brian.

16:46.988 --> 16:50.025
All the most important
Broadway people will be there.

16:50.059 --> 16:52.894
Playwrights, money men,
even Stanley Kowalski.

16:52.929 --> 16:54.063
Hi. I'm Michael.

16:54.097 --> 16:56.482
Michael!

16:56.517 --> 16:58.735
Michael!

16:58.770 --> 17:00.771
Um, and this is my wife, Nancy.

17:00.822 --> 17:02.890
Nancy!

17:02.908 --> 17:05.760
Nancy!

17:05.795 --> 17:09.198
And this is our Uzbekistani
friend, Bokyavroychesku.

17:09.232 --> 17:10.366
Hi.

17:17.639 --> 17:19.644
Stewie, don't you think you're
overdoing it with that outfit.

17:19.669 --> 17:22.119
I don't wear anything I can't
take off with a flourish.

17:25.508 --> 17:27.208
Wow, this is amazing.

17:27.243 --> 17:29.011
Every major
playwright is here.

17:29.045 --> 17:31.147
Yes, and it seems to be
quite a successful party.

17:31.181 --> 17:33.450
Several of them have
already committed suicide.

17:33.484 --> 17:34.551
Great party.

17:34.569 --> 17:35.903
Totally!

17:36.955 --> 17:37.989
MAN:
Hey, Tony.

17:38.023 --> 17:39.691
Oh, my God,
that's David Mamet.

17:39.725 --> 17:40.926
There's the man
of the evening.

17:40.944 --> 17:43.662
<i>I read your play,
An American Marriage.</i>

17:43.697 --> 17:46.649
It was incredibly raw.
I was pretty damn impressed.

17:46.684 --> 17:47.717
Oh, thank you, David.

17:47.752 --> 17:49.804
David, this is Brian.
Brian, David.

17:49.838 --> 17:50.788
Oh, it's an honor.

17:50.822 --> 17:52.089
Listen, I just
want to say

17:52.124 --> 17:53.925
<i>what an inspiration
Glengarry Glen Ross was to me</i>

17:53.960 --> 17:55.394
when I was writing
my own play.

17:55.428 --> 17:56.645
Easy.

17:56.679 --> 17:58.014
Is that
Tony Dovolani?

17:58.048 --> 17:59.549
Hello. Alan Bennett.

17:59.583 --> 18:01.084
Fantastic play.

18:01.118 --> 18:02.753
And I'm
Yasmina Reza.

18:02.787 --> 18:04.455
Oh, are you his night nurse?

18:04.489 --> 18:05.990
No, I'm
a playwright.

18:06.025 --> 18:07.659
<i>I wrote Art and
God of Carnage.</i>

18:07.693 --> 18:09.027
I won
a Pulitzer.

18:09.061 --> 18:11.464
You want to say your
grade point average, too?

18:11.498 --> 18:13.566
Boy, it's just
so weird to be in a room

18:13.600 --> 18:15.769
where every single person
in the room is a playwright.

18:15.803 --> 18:17.270
Every single
one of us.

18:17.305 --> 18:18.406
Everyone.

18:18.440 --> 18:19.891
Including me.

18:19.925 --> 18:21.492
I'm a playwright.

18:21.527 --> 18:23.362
Brian, my God,
take it down a notch.

18:23.396 --> 18:25.064
You're a playwright?
In New York?

18:25.098 --> 18:26.732
Well, Quahog,
Rhode Island.

18:26.766 --> 18:28.251
You're kidding.

18:28.285 --> 18:29.986
We just went and saw a play
in Quahog, Rhode Island.

18:30.020 --> 18:31.188
Really?

18:31.223 --> 18:33.624
<i>Have you ever heard
of A Passing Fancy?</i>

18:33.642 --> 18:34.675
I have!

18:34.726 --> 18:37.162
Oh, my God,
what a load of rubbish.

18:37.196 --> 18:38.196
(chuckles)

18:38.231 --> 18:39.532
We have this thing
where we go

18:39.566 --> 18:41.033
to the worst regional theater
we can find

18:41.068 --> 18:42.935
and laugh ourselves sick.

18:42.970 --> 18:45.472
And this one took
the grand prize.

18:45.490 --> 18:47.491
(laughing)

18:47.525 --> 18:48.610
Really?

18:48.644 --> 18:51.112
I-I had heard
only good things.

18:51.147 --> 18:54.483
It was a (bleep) piece
of (bleep) on a (bleep)!

18:54.518 --> 18:55.751
(laughing)

18:55.786 --> 18:56.837
Oh, my God.

18:56.871 --> 18:59.723
I got to hear David Mamet
curse in person.

19:09.737 --> 19:11.521
Oh, there you are.

19:11.555 --> 19:14.158
That was the worst
night of my life.

19:14.192 --> 19:16.393
It's not like you were
the only fat guy in there.

19:16.428 --> 19:17.444
What?

19:17.479 --> 19:19.347
Oh, this is about
the play. Sorry.

19:19.382 --> 19:22.885
I was so happy until
I read your play, Stewie.

19:22.920 --> 19:26.088
I finally had some kind
of success as a writer.

19:26.123 --> 19:27.925
It wasn't big,
but it was mine.

19:27.959 --> 19:30.227
I was fine being an okay writer,

19:30.261 --> 19:33.331
until I discovered I was living
in a house with a brilliant one.

19:33.365 --> 19:35.701
I know we don't talk
about doggy years a lot,

19:35.735 --> 19:38.037
but I-I just wish
you could have waited

19:38.071 --> 19:41.474
to find out how talented
you were until I was gone.

19:41.509 --> 19:43.778
By the time I'm dead,
you won't even be ten.

19:43.812 --> 19:45.913
You'll have 70 more years
to be great.

19:45.948 --> 19:50.052
I just wish I could have
had five to be good.

19:50.086 --> 19:51.888
There's your voice, Brian.

19:51.922 --> 19:54.257
It's a depressing voice,
but it's yours.

19:54.291 --> 19:55.658
Write from that.

19:55.693 --> 19:57.561
Listen, me and
the other playwrights

19:57.596 --> 19:58.796
are going out
to have drinks

19:58.830 --> 20:00.448
with Woody Allen
and his daughter-wife.

20:00.483 --> 20:01.633
You want to come?

20:01.667 --> 20:02.834
All right.

20:02.869 --> 20:05.805
We can just sit here
for a while.

20:07.841 --> 20:11.645
They talked a lot more crap
about you after you left.

20:18.121 --> 20:20.806
Oh, God, that was just terrible.
Terrible.

20:20.857 --> 20:22.542
I don't understand.

20:22.577 --> 20:24.644
Stewie, what happened
to the play you wrote?

20:24.679 --> 20:26.013
That was terrible.

20:26.048 --> 20:27.288
Well, I gave it
a final polish..

20:27.316 --> 20:28.516
Changed a few things.

20:28.550 --> 20:30.452
Guess I kind of mucked
the whole thing up.

20:30.486 --> 20:32.588
But how? You...

20:32.622 --> 20:33.589
Wait

20:33.623 --> 20:36.159
Did you do that... for me?

20:36.210 --> 20:37.894
What are you talking about?
I'm a young writer.

20:37.928 --> 20:39.763
I just wasn't as ready
as I thought I was.

20:39.798 --> 20:42.266
Maybe in a couple of years,
who knows?

20:42.300 --> 20:44.402
Stewie, you shouldn't
have done that.

20:44.437 --> 20:47.073
But... thanks.

20:47.107 --> 20:48.841
No problem, Brian.

20:48.875 --> 20:50.349
Hey, what do you say
we head back home?

20:50.374 --> 20:51.311
That sounds good.

20:51.810 --> 20:54.429
You know, I'm going to move to
New York City when I'm older,

20:54.463 --> 20:55.898
'cause it's
twinkly and big

20:55.932 --> 20:57.349
and this is where
dreams come true,

20:57.400 --> 20:59.351
and that giant bird's
coming for us, isn't he?

20:59.352 --> 22:00.352
Thank you for watching "Family Guy" on
www.FOX-fan.ru